# POML VS Code Extension — Release & Registry Checklist

## Pre-Release Checklist

### Code Quality
- [ ] All unit tests passing (`npm test`)
- [ ] All E2E tests passing
- [ ] Linting passes (`npm run lint`)
- [ ] Code coverage ≥50% (target ~80%)
- [ ] No console errors or warnings in debug mode
- [ ] TypeScript strict mode passes

### Binary & Packaging
- [ ] All 6 platform binaries built and tested
  - [ ] macOS amd64
  - [ ] macOS arm64
  - [ ] Linux amd64
  - [ ] Linux arm64
  - [ ] Windows amd64
  - [ ] Windows arm64
- [ ] Binary size reasonable (~15 MB per platform)
- [ ] Binaries executable and functional on target platforms
- [ ] `.vsix` package builds successfully

### Documentation
- [ ] README.md updated with latest features
- [ ] Extension icon (128×128, PNG) added to `resources/icon.png`
- [ ] Marketplace screenshots captured (1200×750 minimum)
- [ ] CHANGELOG updated with new features
- [ ] Quickstart guide updated
- [ ] E2E plan updated with actual metrics

### Configuration
- [ ] Version bumped in `package.json` (e.g., `0.1.0` → `0.1.1`)
- [ ] package-lock.json updated (`npm install`)
- [ ] License field correct (MIT)
- [ ] Repository URL correct
- [ ] Publisher name correct

### Functionality
- [ ] Extension auto-activates on startup
- [ ] Status bar indicator shows and updates correctly
- [ ] `poml_validate` tool works
- [ ] `poml_convert` tool works
- [ ] `poml_search` tool works
- [ ] `poml_patch` tool works
- [ ] `poml_diagram` tool works
- [ ] `poml_get_ast` tool works
- [ ] Error messages are helpful and actionable
- [ ] Server gracefully handles missing binary

---

## Release Steps

### 1. Local Build & Test
```bash
# Build binaries
bash scripts/build-binaries.sh

# Install dependencies
cd vscode-extension
npm install

# Run tests
npm test

# Build extension
npm run vscode:prepublish

# Package VSIX
npm run package

# Test VSIX locally in VS Code
# Extensions → Install from VSIX → select poml-mcp-*.vsix
```

### 2. Commit & Tag
```bash
# Commit all changes
git add .
git commit -m "chore: release v0.1.0"

# Create tag
git tag v0.1.0

# Push to origin
git push origin main --tags
```

### 3. GitHub Actions (Automated)
- GitHub Actions triggers automatically on tag push
- Builds all binaries in parallel
- Packages extension
- Creates GitHub Release with `.vsix` artifact
- Publishes to marketplaces (if tokens configured)

### 4. Verify Release
- [ ] GitHub Release created with changelog
- [ ] `.vsix` artifact available on release page
- [ ] VS Code Marketplace shows new version (may take 24 hours)
- [ ] Open VSX shows new version (typically within 1 hour)

---

## VS Code Marketplace Publication

### Prerequisites
- VS Code Marketplace account (requires Microsoft account)
- Personal Access Token (PAT) with `Marketplace` scope
- Publisher account verified

### Setup
```bash
# Create publisher (one-time)
npx vsce create-publisher atlas-foundry

# Login (one-time)
npx vsce login atlas-foundry
```

### Publish (Manual)
```bash
# From vscode-extension directory
npm run package

# Publish
npx vsce publish
```

### Publish (Automated via GitHub Actions)
1. Set secret: `VSCODE_MARKETPLACE_TOKEN` in GitHub repository
2. Tag commit: `git tag v0.1.0 && git push origin --tags`
3. GitHub Actions automatically publishes

---

## Open VSX Registry Publication

### Prerequisites
- Open VSX account (simple signup at https://open-vsx.org)
- Personal Access Token (PAT)

### Setup
```bash
# Publish (automated via GitHub Actions)
# Set secret: OVSX_TOKEN in GitHub repository
```

### Manual Publish
```bash
npx ovsx publish -p $OVSX_TOKEN
```

---

## MCP Registry Registration

### Prerequisites
- MCP Registry account (https://registry.modelcontextprotocol.io)
- Server must be published to npm or available via GitHub

### Steps
1. Navigate to https://registry.modelcontextprotocol.io/submit
2. Fill out form:
   - **Name:** POML MCP Server
   - **Type:** Server
   - **Repository:** https://github.com/atlas-foundry/poml-go-sdk
   - **Installation:** `npx @atlas-foundry/poml-mcp`
   - **Description:** POML (Prompt Orchestration Markup Language) server with VS Code extension
3. Submit for review (~1–2 weeks)
4. Verify listing on registry

---

## GitHub Copilot Marketplace (Optional)

### Eligibility
- Extension must be available on VS Code Marketplace
- Must have ≥50 installs and ≥4.0 rating (typically)
- Auto-registered if meets criteria

### Verification
1. Check https://copilot.github.com/marketplace
2. Verify extension appears in Copilot recommendations
3. No additional submission required (automatic indexing)

---

## Post-Release Monitoring

### Metrics to Track
- [ ] Installation count (daily, weekly, monthly)
- [ ] Rating and review count
- [ ] Issue reports (bug fixes prioritized)
- [ ] Feature requests (roadmap planning)
- [ ] User feedback and testimonials

### Support Plan
- [ ] GitHub Issues: Triage and respond within 24 hours
- [ ] Discussions: Address questions in community tab
- [ ] Social media: Announce on Twitter/LinkedIn
- [ ] Blog: Post case studies from early adopters

### Update Schedule
- [ ] Bug fixes: Release as needed
- [ ] Features: Release every 2–4 weeks
- [ ] Major versions: Coordinate with Go SDK releases

---

## Rollback Plan

### If Critical Issue Found
1. Unpublish from VS Code Marketplace
   ```bash
   npx vsce unpublish --publisher atlas-foundry --extension poml-mcp --version 0.1.0
   ```
2. Unpublish from Open VSX
   ```bash
   curl -X DELETE https://open-vsx.org/api/-/unpublish/atlas-foundry/poml-mcp/0.1.0 \
     -H "Authorization: Bearer $OVSX_TOKEN"
   ```
3. Create new tag for patched version (e.g., `v0.1.1-hotfix`)
4. Re-release after fix verification

---

## Success Criteria

- [ ] ≥100 installs within 1 month
- [ ] ≥4.5 star rating on VS Code Marketplace
- [ ] &lt;5% of issues related to binary/installation
- [ ] &lt;1 hour median issue response time
- [ ] 100% automated release pipeline
- [ ] No manual steps for CI/CD (tag → publish fully automated)

